1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 { The Dragon variation of the Sicilian gets it's name from the pawn structure and has what's called the "Dragon bishop". This fianchettoed bishop on g7 controls the long diagonal and will play a key part in not only kingside defense but eventually offense.
This variation is very aggressive and a test to see who can withstand the attack on their king while managing to destroy the other's. This, is the dragon. } *


[Event "The Sicilian: The Dragon: The Yugoslav Attack"]
[Date "2018.12.22"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B78"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Dragon Variation, Yugoslav Attack"]
[StudyName "The Sicilian"]
[ChapterName "The Dragon: The Yugoslav Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NGpnogsw/k8kie88Q"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/iiPlasma"]
[UTCDate "2018.12.22"]
[UTCTime "15:26:40"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 { This bishop move to e3 shows white's intentions to initiate the Yugoslav Attack. White will quickly develop queenside, castle long, and go on an all out attack on the black king. } 6... Bg7 (6... Ng4 { In both of the variations shown, we see how this is a horrible blunder. At face value it can look like a fine move by attack the bishop and starting to have some kingside presence, after both sequences black will be down material. } 7. Bb5+ Nc6 (7... Bd7 8. Qxg4) 8. Nxc6 bxc6 9. Bxc6+ Bd7 10. Bxa8) 7. f3 { This now prevents any Ng4 ideas in the future while also preparing Qd2 and castling long. } 7... O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 { Another option for white would be an immediate castle queenside. This would give white extra time for his attack but it also allows black the option of d5 which immediately opens up the center. } { [%cal Gd6d5] } 9... Bd7 10. O-O-O { Here we've reached the standard positions you'll see when playing the Dragon against the Yugoslav attack.
White Strategies:
1) Open the h-file with h2-h4-h5 with support from the g pawn
2) Exchange the dark square bishops with Be3-h6.
3) Eliminate the kingside knight either through methods such as Nd5 and g4-g5
Black Strategies:
1) Moves such as Ra8-c8, Ne5-c4, b7-b5, and Qd8-a5 to generate queenside counterplay.
2) A thematic sacrifice is the rook on c8 taking the knight on c3 to dismantle the pawns around the white king.
3) Defensively, black has ideas such as h7-h5 and sequences where he looks to freeze not only the white kingside pawns but also white's initiative.
Warning: This line is very theoretical and while smart play is useful, you will have to learn how to navigate some of the critical positions that arise in this opening. } *


[Event "The Sicilian: Game 1: Minic vs P. Lee"]
[Date "2018.12.23"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B78"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Dragon Variation, Yugoslav Attack, Old Line"]
[StudyName "The Sicilian"]
[ChapterName "Game 1: Minic vs P. Lee"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NGpnogsw/OfwgugoE"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/iiPlasma"]
[UTCDate "2018.12.23"]
[UTCTime "17:38:39"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O Rc8 11. Bb3 { A sensible move that moves the bishop out of the rooks scope. } 11... Ne5 { Gives the semi-open file for the rook while also preparing Ne5-Nc4 } { [%cal Gc8c1,Ge5c4] } 12. h4 Nc4 { Another possibility is is h5 which freezes the kingside. (Check Game 2 to see this variation) } 13. Bxc4 { It's generally better for white to exchange the light square bishop for the knight. The other bishop can't be exchanged because it has to be used to exchange for the "Dragon Bishop" } 13... Rxc4 14. h5 { Another possibility for white here is g4 instead of the h pawn's sacrifice. } 14... Nxh5 15. g4 { White has opened up the h-file and also gained time for the attack by attacking the knight. } 15... Nf6 16. Bh6 { This move leads to many complications. This is to exchange dark squared bishops but also the start of the attack for white. } 16... Bxh6 { This move is the start of the end, black is giving time to white for his attack. } (16... Nxe4 { This is the theoretically best line for black. Here he threatens d4 and instead of giving into white's attack he is going on the counter attack on white's king now. } 17. Qe3 Rxc3 18. bxc3 Nf6 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 { White is ahead the exchange but Black can now counterattack with Qa5 and Rc8 } 20. Qh6+ Kh8 21. g5 Nh5 { Notice how this now is completely fine for black and White's attack has now reached a dead end. If we were to continue this though: } 22. Rxh5 gxh5 23. Rh1 Rg8 24. Rxh5 Rg7 { Here we see that white's attack is now completely neutralized and black still has his light squared bishop and queen to go on the counterattack against the open white king. }) 17. Qxh6 Rxc3 { He prevents Nd5 while also ruining the pawn structure around the white king. This is a thematic sacrifice for black that is essential to getting counterplay. Unfortunately, this is too late. } 18. g5 { Ignoring the rook and starting an unstoppable attack on the h-file. } 18... Nh5 19. Rxh5 { Black's final kingside defender is wiped off the board and now the kingside is about to crumble. } 19... gxh5 20. Rh1 { It doesn't matter that black is up a rook right now because the attack down the h-file will be the game. } 20... Qc8 (20... f6 21. g6 hxg6 22. Qxg6+ Kh8 23. Rxh5# { Here we see how no matter how black may try and diverge the attacking forces from the kingside, the impeding checkmate is coming soon. }) 21. Rxh5 Bf5 22. exf5 Rxc2+ 23. Nxc2 Qxf5 24. g6 Qxg6 25. Rg5 { Black resigned on move 24 but here we can see why. If he were to continue the game, his queen would be lost and with her the game. } *


