1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 Nc6 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. h4 Rc8 11. Bb3 h5 12. O-O-O Ne5 { Here we have a different move order but it's the same position but here we have h7-h5 which stops white's ideas of h5. } 13. Bg5 { Here white goes to an active square and prepares for a kingside attack. Possible continuations are Bh6, g2-g4 or Kb1. } { [%cal Gg2g4,Gg5h6,Gc1b1] } 13... Rc5 { Not only can the rook pressure the c-file but it can also defend the fourth rank. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ge5c4] } 14. g4 { White goes 'all in' } 14... hxg4 15. Bxf6 { This is a huge mistake for white. Never exchange your dark square bishop for anything but the "Dragon Bishop" unless it leads to something calculated. This goes for both colors. A better move would've been h5 to open the h-file. } 15... Bxf6 16. h5 g5 { Black refuses to open the h-file which is a key part of white's attack. } 17. Nd5 Rxd5 { Since the rook is always sacrificed for the knight, this is a fine time to sacrifice. Although it isn't on c3 where it would open the kingside pawn structure, this is still a great way to remove white's powerful knight. } 18. exd5 gxf3 19. c3 g4 { White's attack is all but extinguished and now black has two passed pawns which he's threatening to push to victory. } 20. Kb1 Qb6 21. Bc2 Nc4 22. Qc1 Rc8 23. Nf5 { This deadly mistake allows black a deadly combination. } 23... Na3+ 24. Ka1 Rxc3 25. Nxe7+ Kf8 { White resigned here but let's keep playing the position to understand why. } 26. bxc3 (26. Rh2 f2 { Another variation showing how if white does prevent mate, his position completely falls apart as the black pawns push to victory. }) 26... Bxc3+ 27. Qb2 Qxb2# *


