1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O { White's idea here instead of Bc4 is to gain time for his kingside attack. If Black just develops normally it can be very advantageous to White. } 9... d5 { This is what happens because Bc4 didn't. } 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Bd4 { Here white is trying to offer the exchange of the dark-squared bishops. } 12... e5 13. Bc5 Be6 { This is a typical exchange in the Dragon. } 14. Ne4 (14. Bxf8 Qxf8 { In this variation most GMs agree that Black's attacking chances and dark square control make up for the material deficit. }) 14... Re8 15. g4 h6 16. h4 a5 17. g5 h5 18. a4 Qc7 19. Bc4 Red8 20. Qf2 Qb7 21. b3 { The game is a lot slower than the last two because both flanks are closed. } 21... Nf4 22. Bxe6 Nxe6 23. Rxd8+ Rxd8 24. Bb6 Ra8 25. Rd1 Nd4 26. Bc5 Qd7 27. Nf6+ Bxf6 28. gxf6 Qf5 (28... Rd8 29. Rd3 Nxb3+ 30. Rxb3 Qd1+ 31. Kb2 Rd2 { This is the correct move that black should've played. This woudl've been a winning attack for him. } 32. Qxd2 Qxd2) 29. Bxd4 exd4 30. Qxd4 Qxf3 31. Qe5 { It's too late now as White is breaking through on the kingside. } 31... Qf2 32. Rd7 Rf8 33. Kb2 c5 34. Rxf7 { Black resigned here but we'll continue to see why exactly. } 34... Kxf7 35. Qe7+ Kg8 36. Qg7# *


