1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be2 Bg7 7. O-O Nc6 8. Nb3 O-O 9. Bg5 a6 { Black begins the queenside operation. } { [%cal Ge2b5] } 10. f4 b5 11. Bf3 { White threatens e4-e5 but black has a natural response. } { [%cal Ge4e5] } 11... Bb7 12. Kh1 { This is a common move where white avoids any tactics or checks with Qb6. } 12... Nd7 13. Rb1 { Defending the b pawn so the knight on c3 can move to d5. } 13... Re8 14. Nd5 { White is starting to pile on the pressure on the weak e7 pawn. } { [%cal Gd5e7,Gg5e7] } 14... f6 { It might seem strange to block the "Dragon Bishop" but there are positional factors that are positive for black here. } 15. Bh4 e6 16. Ne3 g5 17. Bg3 { After some trades in the middle black's dark squared bishop will become powerful again and help create outposts for the knights. } { [%cal Gf4g6] } 17... gxf4 18. Bxf4 Nde5 19. Bh5 Rf8 20. c3 Qe7 21. Qe2 Ng6 22. Bg3 Nce5 23. Nd2 Kh8 24. Rf2 Rad8 25. Rbf1 Bh6 { Now the dark squared bishop is as active as the light squared bishop. } 26. Ng4 Nxg4 27. Bxg4 d5 { Breaking the center and increasing the activity of his pieces. Black is now in control. } 28. Bh5 dxe4 29. Nxe4 f5 30. Bxg6 hxg6 31. Nd6 Ba8 32. Be5+ Kg8 33. Rd1 Rd7 34. Qd3 Rfd8 { This knight might seem strong here but realize it has no safe squares to escape to. } { [%csl Gd6,Rc8,Rb7,Rb5,Rc4,Re4,Rf5,Rf7,Re8] } 35. Qg3 Qg5 36. Qxg5 Bxg5 37. Rd3 Be4 38. Rh3 Rxd6 39. Rh8+ Kf7 40. Rh7+ Ke8 41. Rh8+ Kd7 { Apicella, Manuel - Svidler, Peter, 0-1, Yerevan ol (Men), 1996, https://lichess.org/Qvj6hrwi } { Here white resigned since it would be a rook versus bishop pair endgame which is completely in black's favor. } *


